    "## Step 28 — Claim review and learning-evidence limits\n",
    "\n",
    "### Small source–claim review\n",
    "\n",
    "The table below is an **AI-assisted review of explicit supplied excerpts**, not a student or independent human review. Examples include supported claims, a numerical contradiction and an unsupported inference. Labels are fixed review judgments, not outputs of the notebook's numeric checker. This small, selected set cannot estimate a population hallucination rate. No live financial facts are asserted by these excerpts.\n",
    "\n",
    "The first excerpt reproduces the title from the saved October 19 run's extraction warning (article `700e18f8960a`). The original checker flagged `5` against the word `five`. That is a representation mismatch in this title, not sufficient evidence of a fabricated quantity. Simple zero–nineteen number words are now normalized; scale, units, fiscal periods, spelled-out compound numbers and semantic equivalence still require review. Historical output is not retroactively labeled as a successful revised run.\n",
    "\n",
    "The second table samples current specialist claims and leaves **student review pending**. Students should inspect the original article/provider record and enter their judgment and rationale before claiming manual validation. Evidence paths and copied values alone do not validate interpretations.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Learning evidence\n",
    "\n",
    "Step 27 uses intentional fault injection and predefined lesson categories. It demonstrates persistence, feedback wiring and a changed later draft under controlled conditions. It does not establish general learning or live-model improvement. The fixed-evidence comparison below reports observed audit results without assuming a gain. The default uses deterministic analysis. An optional live comparison is disabled by default, requires live mode and an available model, makes paid specialist calls only (no new retrieval), and still cannot establish general improvement from one pair. Both conditions use the same bundle; model randomness and prior lesson selection remain limitations.\n"
   ]
